\subsection{Position Disruption vs.\ Information Loss}
\label{sec:zeromask}

The preceding results show that $\pi_{0.5}$'s random pruning at $k{=}0.9$ collapses to 9.0\% on Object despite removing only 10\% of tokens. We isolate whether this failure stems from information loss (missing pixel content) or position disruption (shifted token indices in the LLM's sequence).

\textbf{Zero-mask ablation.} We compare two conditions at $k{=}0.9$ with random token selection on $\pi_{0.5}$ Object: (1)~\emph{hard pruning}, which physically removes selected tokens and compacts the remaining sequence; (2)~\emph{zero-masking}, which replaces selected token embeddings with zero vectors while preserving all token positions. Both conditions destroy identical visual information---the same 10\% of vision tokens carry no signal---but only hard pruning alters the positional structure of the remaining sequence.

Zero-masking preserves 98.0\% SR ($-$0.2\,pp from the 98.2\% baseline), while hard pruning collapses to 9.0\% ($-$89.2\,pp). The 89\,pp gap is entirely attributable to position disruption: $\pi_{0.5}$'s linear connector with learned positional encodings creates a rigid position-to-semantics mapping that tolerates complete signal destruction but not index shifts.

This result provides causal evidence for the connector dependence observed in Section~\ref{sec:two_axis}: token-independent connectors do not make tokens dispensable---they make token \emph{positions} critical. The practical implication is that pruning methods for low-CV architectures must either preserve positional structure (e.g., zero-masking, learned compression) or re-encode positions after deletion.

\subsection{When Does Pruning Method Matter? CV as Sensitivity Indicator}
\label{sec:cv_sensitivity}

