\begin{abstract}
Vision-Language-Action (VLA) models encode observations as hundreds of vision tokens, creating a computational bottleneck for real-time robotic control. Token pruning offers a training-free remedy, yet its effect varies dramatically across architectures: at retention ratio $k{=}0.95$, $\pi_{0.5}$ preserves 98\% task success while SmolVLA collapses to 0\%---why?

We identify the vision-language connector as the primary architectural factor governing pruning tolerance. The coefficient of variation (CV) of post-connector token norms, computable in a single forward pass, predicts compressibility across the full architecture spectrum: from CV$\,{=}\,$0.038 (near-immune linear projection) to CV$\,{=}\,$0.314 (catastrophic pixel-shuffle collapse). A controlled pair---$\pi_0$ and OpenVLA, both encoding 256 vision tokens through different connectors---yields CV values of 0.038 vs.\ 0.215, capturing their divergent pruning responses without closed-loop evaluation. Across five VLA architectures (450\,M--7\,B parameters) and four LIBERO manipulation suites, token-independent connectors degrade gracefully, while token-entangling connectors exhibit abrupt cliffs: SmolVLA collapses when just 6 of 128 tokens are removed.

Importance-based selection is not universally beneficial---it can be actively harmful. At moderate compression ($k{=}0.85$), L2 norm selection preserves 98\% success on $\pi_{0.5}$ where random yields 5\% (93\,pp gap); on multi-step Long tasks, removing just 7.5\% of tokens ($k{=}0.925$) produces a 45\,pp scoring gap (L2: 83\% vs.\ random: 39\%); at aggressive ratios, this advantage reverses---random outperforms L2 by $+$36\,pp on $\pi_{0.5}$ ($k{=}0.5$) and $+$27\,pp on X-VLA. Attention-based scoring exhibits a within-model reversal on OpenVLA: $-$12.6\,pp on Object yet $+$0.4\,pp on Spatial at $k{=}0.9$. Even on high-CV architectures where L2 helps on simple manipulation ($+$3--8\,pp on Object), the advantage diminishes or reverses on multi-step tasks---no scoring method is universally safe. Task complexity amplifies these connector-dependent patterns: multi-step tasks magnify entangling-connector losses by up to $20{\times}$, whereas independent connectors show ${\leq}$11\,pp degradation. A zero-mask ablation on $\pi_{0.5}$ isolates the mechanism: zeroing 10\% of tokens preserves 98.0\% SR while physically removing them collapses to 9.0\%---the 89\,pp gap is entirely attributable to position disruption, not information loss. For architectures where all deletion strategies fail, a lightweight re-encoding module (${\sim}$5\% extra parameters) recovers $>$60\% success at 75\% compression.
\end{abstract}

